> In article <1174@cmtl01.UUCP> oisin@cmtl01.UUCP (Oisin) writes:
> >  BTW, I'm having great fun trying to compile 2.3e on my poor beleagered
> >  little XT.  Every two hours it tells me "more than 64K in segment _DATA".
> 
> Which linker are you using? I had the same problem with my tlink 2.0. It said:
> DGROUP: segment/group exceeds 64k. The funny thing is that there IS NO DGROUP!
> Huge memory model doesn't group the static data modules. As far as I can see,
> tlink is trying to combine modules which it shouldn't combine...
> Well, I solved the problem using Microsoft link (segmented executable). It 
> didn't have any problems after I instructed: "/SEGMENTS:400".

At least with Microsoft compilers, static data, constants and stack are
grouped together (even in huge model, I think).  Using huge model will
significantly affect performance;  you don't really need it, since there
are no *single* data items bigger than 64k.  If you still have trouble,
make all of the big static data items "far".  The switch -Gt60 for MSC
will also help.

I've compiled and run Nethack 2.2 with everything except HARD defined,
and with the level size set to 120x43 -- but it's a tight squeeze. 
Nethack 2.3 is probably worse, but it should be possible to compile
it for the PC somehow.

